WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.950
Take every stable nucleus
and plot it as one dot:

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.100
neutrons up the side,
protons along the bottom.

3
00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:08.770
The dots don't scatter.

4
00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:11.580
They crowd into one narrow band.

5
00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:14.550
Light nuclei have about
equal numbers of each.

6
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:16.710
Heavier ones drift above.

7
00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:22.420
Lead-208 has 126 neutrons
but only 82 protons,

8
00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:25.290
and beyond lead, nothing is stable.

9
00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:28.170
So what about a nucleus off the band?

10
00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:31.080
Does its position tell
you how it will decay?

11
00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:35.320
In this mission, you'll find out why
heavy nuclei need extra neutrons to hold

12
00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:36.310
together.

13
00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:41.270
Then you'll follow an excited nickel-60
nucleus as it drops down its energy levels,

14
00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:43.110
giving out gamma rays.

15
00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:47.730
Your mission: predict how a nucleus will
decay from where it sits on the chart.
